---
/**
 * A palm-sized boat you can point, beside the paragraph about pointing boats.
 *
 * saltline's whole model is that the angle between where you want to go and
 * where the wind is coming from decides everything else, and that is one of
 * those things a sentence describes and a dial explains. So: a wind that does
 * not move, a boat that does, and the four numbers the game's own HUD shows
 * changing as you swing it, including the wedge at the top where the sails
 * stop working and you stop with them.
 *
 * The curve is saltline's own point-of-sail function, sampled; the six captures
 * above are the check that it is. Both are built in data/polar.ts.
 *
 * The server renders the whole thing at the opening heading, so the figure is
 * correct and readable before any script runs and there is nothing to flash.
 * The script re-runs the same functions on the same table; the table travels
 * to it as JSON on the element rather than as an import, which keeps saltline's
 * prose and the model that built the table out of a bundle that only needs the
 * pairs of numbers it came out as.
 */
import {
  checks,
  checkWorst,
  fullSailN,
  polar,
  polarLerpError,
  polarSaid,
  polarStep,
} from '../data/polar';
import { bestVmg, kn, said, sail, saidVmg, sailPath } from '../lib/polar';

/** Points of thrust, the unit both agreements below are quoted in. */
const pt = (n: number): string => n.toFixed(1);

/* Signed, and built here rather than in the template, because Astro keeps the
   line break between two expressions and would put a space after the sign. */
const outBy = (c: (typeof checks)[number]): string => {
  const d = (c.table - c.hud) * 100;
  return `${d < 0 ? '-' : '+'}${pt(Math.abs(d))}`;
};

/* Beam reach: the fastest thing the boat does, and far enough from the no-go
   wedge that the first drag in either direction shows something changing. */
const OPEN = 105;

const C = 120;
const R = 92;
const best = bestVmg(polar);
const now = sail(polar, OPEN);

/** Degrees clockwise from straight up, which is where the wind comes from. */
const at = (deg: number, r: number): [number, number] => {
  const t = (deg * Math.PI) / 180;
  return [+(C + r * Math.sin(t)).toFixed(2), +(C - r * Math.cos(t)).toFixed(2)];
};

const wedge = `M${C} ${C} L${at(-polar.noGo, R).join(' ')} A${R} ${R} 0 0 1 ${at(polar.noGo, R).join(' ')} Z`;

const TICKS = [30, 60, 90, 120, 150, 180, -30, -60, -90, -120, -150];

const rows: Array<[string, string]> = [
  ['Off the wind', `${Math.abs(now.rel)}°`],
  ['Point of sail', now.point],
  ['Thrust', `${Math.round(now.thrust * 100)}%`],
  ['Boat speed', `${kn(now.speed)} kn`],
  ['VMG', saidVmg(now.vmg)],
];
---

<figure
  class="wt"
  data-wt
  data-polar={JSON.stringify({ noGo: polar.noGo, hull: polar.hull, rows: polar.rows })}
  data-best={best.toFixed(3)}
>
  <div class="wt__body">
    <div class="wt__dial" data-wt-dial>
      <svg
        viewBox="0 0 240 240"
        role="img"
        aria-label="A boat on a dial, with the wind coming from the top and a wedge marking the angles too close to it to sail."
      >
        <circle class="wt__ring" cx={C} cy={C} r={R}></circle>
        <path class="wt__nogo" d={wedge}></path>

        {
          TICKS.map((t) => {
            const [x1, y1] = at(t, R);
            const [x2, y2] = at(t, t % 90 === 0 ? R - 11 : R - 6);
            return <line class="wt__tick" x1={x1} y1={y1} x2={x2} y2={y2} />;
          })
        }

        {/* The wind, which never moves. Everything on the dial is read off it. */}
        <g class="wt__wind">
          <line x1={C} y1="6" x2={C} y2="22"></line>
          <path d={`M${C - 4} 19 L${C} 27 L${C + 4} 19 Z`}></path>
        </g>

        <g class="wt__boat" data-wt-boat transform={`rotate(${OPEN} ${C} ${C})`}>
          {
            /* Thrust, off the bow, as long as the polar says it is. At nothing
              it is not a short arrow, it is no arrow. */
          }
          <g class="wt__thrust" data-wt-thrust data-wt-dead={now.thrust < 0.005 ? 'yes' : null}>
            <line x1={C} y1="76" x2={C} y2={+(76 - now.thrust * 40).toFixed(2)}></line>
            <path
              d={`M${C - 4} ${(80 - now.thrust * 40).toFixed(2)} L${C} ${(72 - now.thrust * 40).toFixed(2)} L${C + 4} ${(80 - now.thrust * 40).toFixed(2)} Z`}
            ></path>
          </g>

          <path
            class="wt__hull"
            d={`M${C} 80 L${C + 9} 108 L${C + 6} 140 L${C - 6} 140 L${C - 9} 108 Z`}></path>

          <g class="wt__rig" data-wt-sail transform={`rotate(${now.sheet.toFixed(1)} ${C} 100)`}>
            <path
              class="wt__sail"
              data-wt-luff={now.luffing ? 'yes' : null}
              d={sailPath(now.lee, C)}></path>
            <line class="wt__boom" x1={C} y1="100" x2={C} y2="143"></line>
          </g>
          <circle class="wt__mast" cx={C} cy="100" r="2.4"></circle>
        </g>

        {
          (
            /* Made good along the wind axis: how much of the boat's speed is
             going the way the wind is not. Up is to windward.

             Over the boat rather than under it. It is a reading rather than a
             part of the hull, which argued for putting it behind, but on a
             beam reach the hull lies across the axis and a reading you cannot
             see is not one. It carries its own dark edge instead. */
            <g class="wt__vmg" data-wt-vmg>
              <line x1={C} y1={C} x2={C} y2={+(C - (now.vmg / best) * (R - 14)).toFixed(2)} />
            </g>
          )
        }
      </svg>
    </div>

    <dl class="wt__read" data-wt-read>
      {
        rows.map(([k, v]) => (
          <div>
            <dt>{k}</dt>
            <dd>{v}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
  </div>

  <label class="range" for="wt-heading">
    <span>
      Heading <output for="wt-heading" data-wt-out aria-hidden="true">{now.point}</output>
    </span>
    <input
      id="wt-heading"
      type="range"
      min="-180"
      max="180"
      step="1"
      value={OPEN}
      data-wt-range
      aria-valuetext={said(now)}
    />
  </label>

  <details class="wt__table">
    <summary
      >The real polar, all {polar.rows.length} rows, and the six captures that check it</summary
    >

    <p>
      The six frames above were read off the game's own HUD long before this curve was on the page.
      Each one is a heading the game sailed and a thrust it reported at that heading, so laying them
      beside what this table answers at the same angle is the only check that matters. The worst of
      the six is {pt(checkWorst)} of a point out.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Frame</th>
          <th scope="col">Off the wind</th>
          <th scope="col">HUD</th>
          <th scope="col">Here</th>
          <th scope="col">Out by</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          checks.map((c) => (
            <tr data-from="capture">
              <td>{c.at}</td>
              <td>{c.rel}&deg;</td>
              <td>{Math.round(c.hud * 100)}%</td>
              <td>{pt(c.table * 100)}%</td>
              <td>{outBy(c)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      The curve itself is saltline's <code>pointOfSail</code> function, ported unchanged and evaluated
      every {polarStep} degrees. The figure joins the rows with straight lines, which is a shortcut, and
      it is worth {pt(polarLerpError)} of a point at its worst: smaller than the captures' own agreement
      above, so the shortcut is not what is holding the check together. Thrust of 1 is full sail, which
      is {fullSailN.toLocaleString('en-US')} newtons in the game. Boat speed is thrust times {
        kn(polar.hull)
      } knots, the mean of speed over thrust across those same six captures and within a knot of every
      one of them. Below {polar.noGo}&deg; the sails luff and thrust is nothing, and that angle is
      the game's.
    </p>
    <table class="wt__curve">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Off the wind</th>
          <th scope="col">Thrust</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          polar.rows.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{r.rel}&deg;</td>
              <td>{pt(r.thrust * 100)}%</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </details>

  <figcaption class="wt__cap">{polarSaid}</figcaption>
</figure>
